==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 grotty.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒti|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
Clipping of {{m|en|grotesque}} {{suffix|en||y}}. {{piecewise doublet|en|grody}}, the American equivalent. Compare {{m+|enm|groti}}, {{m|enm|grooti|t=slimy, muddy}}, from {{m+|ang|grotiġ|t=earthy, dirty}} (modern dialectal {{ncog|en|groaty}}, {{cog|sco|grotty}}), which is equivalent to {{suf|en|groat|-y|id2=adjectival|nocat=1}}. Compare also {{ncog|gd|grod|t=rotten, lousy, terrible}}, {{ncog|mga|grot|t=bitter, sour}}<!--though etymonline considers the similarity merely superficial/incidental-->.

Originated or popularised by the 1964 film ''[[w:A Hard Day's Night (film)|A Hard Day's Night]]'', starring [[w:The Beatles|the Beatles]] and written by [[w:Alun Owen|Alun Owen]].

